Topic A: Comparison Operators
These operators compare values in MongoDB.
Operator	Meaning
$gt	Greater than
$lt	Less than
$gte	Greater than or equal to
$lte	Less than or equal to
$ne	Not equal to


Q6(a). Display all students whose age is greater than 20.
db.students.find({ age: { $gt: 20 } })

Q6(b). Display students whose marks are less than 75.
db.students.find({ marks: { $lt: 75 } })

Q6(c). Display students whose age is greater than or equal to 21.
db.students.find({ age: { $gte: 21 } })

Q6(d). Display students whose marks are less than or equal to 80.
db.students.find({ marks: { $lte: 80 } })

Q6(e). Display students whose city is not Pune.
db.students.find({ city: { $ne: "Pune" } })

Easy trick to remember:
- $gt = Greater than
- $lt = Less than
- $gte = Greater than or equal to
- $lte = Less than or equal to
- $ne = Not equal
Topic B: Logical Operators
Operator	Meaning
$and	All conditions must be true
$or	At least one condition must be true
$not	Negates a condition
$nor	None of the conditions must be true


Q6(f). Display students whose age is greater than 20 AND marks are greater than 80.
db.students.find({
  $and: [
    { age: { $gt: 20 } },
    { marks: { $gt: 80 } }
  ]
})

Shorter alternative:
db.students.find({
  age: { $gt: 20 },
  marks: { $gt: 80 }
})

Both conditions must be satisfied.
Q6(g). Display students whose city is Pune OR Mumbai.
db.students.find({
  $or: [
    { city: "Pune" },
    { city: "Mumbai" }
  ]
})

Q6(h). Display students whose age is NOT greater than 20.
db.students.find({
  age: { $not: { $gt: 20 } }
})

This includes students aged 20 or younger.
Q6(i). Display students whose course is MCA AND city is Pune.
db.students.find({
  course: "MCA",
  city: "Pune"
})

Q6(j). Display students whose marks are greater than 90 OR age is less than 19.
db.students.find({
  $or: [
    { marks: { $gt: 90 } },
    { age: { $lt: 19 } }
  ]
})

Exam tip: Conditions on different fields are automatically combined using AND when written in the same query document.
Topic C: Projection
Projection controls which fields appear in the result.
- 1 means include a field.
- 0 means exclude a field.
- _id is included by default unless explicitly excluded.
Q6(k). Display only the name of all students.
db.students.find({}, { name: 1, _id: 0 })

Q6(l). Display only the name and age of all students.
db.students.find({}, {
  name: 1,
  age: 1,
  _id: 0
})

Q6(m). Display all fields except the city.
db.students.find({}, { city: 0 })

Q6(n). Display only the name and marks, excluding the _id field.
db.students.find({}, {
  name: 1,
  marks: 1,
  _id: 0
})

Q6(o). Display only the names of students whose age is greater than 20.
db.students.find(
  { age: { $gt: 20 } },
  { name: 1, _id: 0 }
)

Easy trick: find(condition, projection).

The first object filters records. The second object controls which fields are displayed.
Part 4: Sorting, Limiting, Skipping and Counting
Q7(a). Display all students in ascending order of marks.
db.students.find().sort({ marks: 1 })

1 means ascending order.
Q7(b). Display all students in descending order of age.
db.students.find().sort({ age: -1 })

-1 means descending order.
Q7(c). Display the first 5 students.
db.students.find().limit(5)

Q7(d). Display the top 3 students with the highest marks.
db.students.find().sort({ marks: -1 }).limit(3)

Logic: Sort marks from highest to lowest, then display three records.
Q7(e). Skip the first 2 students and display the remaining records.
db.students.find().skip(2)

Q7(f). Display the 4th to 6th students using skip() and limit().
db.students.find().skip(3).limit(3)

Why skip(3)? Because the first three records are skipped, and the next three are displayed.
Q7(g). Count the total number of students.
db.students.countDocuments({})

Q7(h). Count students whose marks are greater than 70.
db.students.countDocuments({
  marks: { $gt: 70 }
})

Q7(i). Display all unique cities.
db.students.distinct("city")

Q7(j). Display all unique courses.
db.students.distinct("course")

Q7(k). Display the top 5 students by marks, showing only name and marks.
db.students.find(
  {},
  { name: 1, marks: 1, _id: 0 }
).sort({ marks: -1 }).limit(5)

Q7(l). Count MCA students and display the unique cities where MCA students belong.
Count MCA students:
db.students.countDocuments({ course: "MCA" })

Display unique cities of MCA students:
db.students.distinct("city", { course: "MCA" })

Part 5: MongoDB Update Operators
Collection: students

These questions use student Roll No. 101.

Important: The following operations modify the database. Execute them in the specified order if you want to observe their combined effects.
Q1. $set — Update marks of the student with Roll No. 101 to 90.
db.students.updateOne(
  { rollNo: 101 },
  { $set: { marks: 90 } }
)

Meaning: Sets the marks field to 90.
Q2. $unset — Remove the city field from the student whose Roll No. is 101.
db.students.updateOne(
  { rollNo: 101 },
  { $unset: { city: "" } }
)

Meaning: Removes the city field entirely.
Q3. $inc — Increase the fees of the student with Roll No. 101 by ₹5,000.
db.students.updateOne(
  { rollNo: 101 },
  { $inc: { fees: 5000 } }
)

Meaning: Adds 5,000 to the existing fees.
Q4. $mul — Double the fees of the student with Roll No. 101.
db.students.updateOne(
  { rollNo: 101 },
  { $mul: { fees: 2 } }
)

Meaning: Multiplies the existing fees by 2.
Q5. $rename — Rename the field marks to score for Roll No. 101.
db.students.updateOne(
  { rollNo: 101 },
  { $rename: { marks: "score" } }
)

Meaning: Renames the field. Its value is preserved.

Note: $rename applies to the specified document's update operation, but the field name change affects the document's schema, so the document will have score instead of marks.
Q6. $min — Update CGPA to 7.5 only if the new value is smaller than the existing value.
db.students.updateOne(
  { rollNo: 101 },
  { $min: { cgpa: 7.5 } }
)

Meaning: If the existing CGPA is greater than 7.5, it becomes 7.5. Otherwise, it remains unchanged.
Q7. $max — Update marks to 95 only if the new value is greater than the existing value.
db.students.updateOne(
  { rollNo: 101 },
  { $max: { marks: 95 } }
)

Meaning: Updates marks to 95 only if the existing value is lower than 95.

Important: If you previously renamed marks to score, this query creates or updates marks separately. Run the $rename question again on a fresh document if you want to demonstrate it independently.
Q8. $currentDate — Add or update lastModified with the current date and time.
db.students.updateOne(
  { rollNo: 101 },
  { $currentDate: { lastModified: true } }
)

Meaning: Sets lastModified to the current date and time.
Quick Revision: Update Operators
Operator	Function
$set	Set a value
$unset	Remove a field
$inc	Increase or decrease a number
$mul	Multiply a number
$rename	Rename a field
$min	Keep the smaller value
$max	Keep the larger value
$currentDate	Set the current date/time


Part 6: MongoDB Indexing
Collection: students

An index helps MongoDB find records more efficiently.
Q1. Create an ascending index on the name field.
db.students.createIndex({ name: 1 })

Expected index name:
name_1

Q2. Create a compound index on department ascending and marks descending.
db.students.createIndex({
  department: 1,
  marks: -1
})

Expected index name:
department_1_marks_-1

Note: Your original schema lists course, not department. The sample dataset in this manual contains both fields, so this query will work. If your actual dataset contains only course, replace department with course.
Q3. Display all indexes created on the students collection.
db.students.getIndexes()

The result includes the default _id_ index and any additional indexes.
Q4. Drop the index created on the name field and verify that it has been removed.
Step 1: Drop the index.
db.students.dropIndex("name_1")

Step 2: Verify.
db.students.getIndexes()

The name_1 index should no longer appear.

Quick revision:
- Create index: createIndex()
- View indexes: getIndexes()
- Delete index: dropIndex()
- Ascending: 1
- Descending: -1
Part 7: MongoDB Aggregation Pipeline
Collection: students

Aggregation is used to filter, group, and summarize data.

Your assignment specifies the operators $match, $group, $push, and $$ROOT. The solutions below use those where relevant, along with $sum to count records.
Q1. Display all students who scored more than 70 marks using $match.
db.students.aggregate([
  {
    $match: { marks: { $gt: 70 } }
  }
])

Meaning: Filters students with marks greater than 70.
Q2. Group students by course and count the total students.
db.students.aggregate([
  {
    $group: {
      _id: "$course",
      totalStudents: { $sum: 1 }
    }
  }
])

Q3. Group students by city and count the number of students in each city.
db.students.aggregate([
  {
    $group: {
      _id: "$city",
      totalStudents: { $sum: 1 }
    }
  }
])

Q4. Display departments having more than 2 students.
db.students.aggregate([
  {
    $group: {
      _id: "$department",
      totalStudents: { $sum: 1 }
    }
  },
  {
    $match: {
      totalStudents: { $gt: 2 }
    }
  }
])

Important correction: The question says more than 2 students, so the condition must be $gt: 2, not $gt: 5.
Q5. Group students by department and display the names of all students.
db.students.aggregate([
  {
    $group: {
      _id: "$department",
      studentNames: { $push: "$name" }
    }
  }
])

Q6. Group students by city and store complete student documents in an array.
db.students.aggregate([
  {
    $group: {
      _id: "$city",
      students: { $push: "$$ROOT" }
    }
  }
])

Remember:
- $push adds values to an array.
- $$ROOT represents the complete current document.
Q7. Display students from the Computer department, then group by city and count students.
db.students.aggregate([
  {
    $match: { department: "Computer" }
  },
  {
    $group: {
      _id: "$city",
      totalStudents: { $sum: 1 }
    }
  }
])

Q8. Group students by department and display the total number and complete student records.
db.students.aggregate([
  {
    $group: {
      _id: "$department",
      totalStudents: { $sum: 1 },
      students: { $push: "$$ROOT" }
    }
  }
])

Q9. Display students with marks greater than 75, group by department, and store names in an array.
db.students.aggregate([
  {
    $match: { marks: { $gt: 75 } }
  },
  {
    $group: {
      _id: "$department",
      studentNames: { $push: "$name" }
    }
  }
])

Q10. Group students by city, display the count and complete records, and show only cities with more than 3 students.
db.students.aggregate([
  {
    $group: {
      _id: "$city",
      totalStudents: { $sum: 1 },
      students: { $push: "$$ROOT" }
    }
  },
  {
    $match: {
      totalStudents: { $gt: 3 }
    }
  }
])

Easy way to remember aggregation:
Stage or operator	Purpose
$match	Filter records
$group	Group records
$sum: 1	Count records
$push	Create an array
$$ROOT	Store the complete document


Execution format:
db.students.aggregate([
  { $match: { /* condition */ } },
  { $group: { /* grouping logic */ } }
])

You can use this structure as a template for many aggregation questions.
